WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.928
An appliance shop sells fridges at twelve hundred manat and washing machines

00:05.028 --> 00:08.568
at nine hundred. In its peak hour it sells zero, one or

00:08.668 --> 00:12.869
two of each, and the two numbers are related. What revenue should

00:12.969 --> 00:15.913
it expect, and do the two sales move together?

00:18.000 --> 00:21.408
Both answers are averages over the joint distribution, one of

00:21.508 --> 00:24.169
a revenue, the other of a product of deviations.

00:25.266 --> 00:28.539
Here is the joint table. Each cell now carries two

00:28.639 --> 00:32.991
numbers: its probability, and the revenue it would produce, twelve

00:33.091 --> 00:36.634
hundred times fridges plus nine hundred times washers.

00:36.733 --> 00:39.940
The expected revenue is what you'd get by weighting each

00:40.040 --> 00:43.483
cell's revenue by its probability and adding up. Nine cells,

00:43.583 --> 00:46.377
and the answer is fifteen hundred and nine manat.

00:46.466 --> 00:49.654
But there's a shortcut. The expectation of a sum is

00:49.754 --> 00:53.199
the sum of the expectations, always, whether or not the

00:53.299 --> 00:57.711
variables are related. Twelve hundred times the expected fridges, plus

00:57.811 --> 01:01.901
nine hundred times the expected washers: the same fifteen hundred

01:02.001 --> 01:04.286
and nine, from the two margins alone.

01:05.383 --> 01:08.777
Now, do two variables move together? Take monthly returns

01:08.877 --> 01:11.474
on two bank shares. Each dot is one possible

01:11.574 --> 01:14.478
pair of returns, and its size is the probability.

01:14.566 --> 01:18.876
Draw cross-hairs through the two means. From the centre to each dot,

01:18.976 --> 01:23.156
draw a rectangle. When both returns are above their means, or both

01:23.256 --> 01:27.371
below, the rectangle is green and counts as positive. When one is

01:27.471 --> 01:31.132
up and the other is down, it's red and counts as negative.

01:31.216 --> 01:35.514
Covariance is the probability-weighted average of these signed areas.

01:35.614 --> 01:38.638
Here the big, likely rectangles are green, so the

01:38.738 --> 01:42.016
covariance is positive: three point two four. The two

01:42.116 --> 01:44.439
shares tend to rise and fall together.

01:45.533 --> 01:48.489
The size of a covariance depends on units: measure

01:48.589 --> 01:51.483
returns in basis points and it grows ten thousand

01:51.583 --> 01:55.150
times. Dividing by the two standard deviations removes that,

01:55.250 --> 01:58.390
and gives the correlation, about zero point five two.

01:59.483 --> 02:03.809
One warning. An energy desk holds short, flat or long positions in oil

02:03.909 --> 02:08.046
and gas, but is never flat in both. The corner rectangles are equal

02:08.146 --> 02:12.157
and opposite, so they cancel, and the covariance is exactly zero.

02:12.250 --> 02:16.035
Yet the positions are clearly dependent: learn that oil

02:16.135 --> 02:19.285
is flat, and you know gas is not. Independence

02:19.385 --> 02:23.383
forces zero covariance, but zero covariance does not force

02:23.483 --> 02:27.692
independence. Covariance only sees straight-line co-movement.

02:29.783 --> 02:34.169
So: an expectation over two variables weights every cell by its

02:34.269 --> 02:39.225
probability. The expectation of a sum splits, always. Covariance is the

02:39.325 --> 02:44.565
average signed rectangle around the means, and correlation is its unit-free

02:44.665 --> 02:48.909
version. And zero covariance is not the same as independence.
